        <p class="caption">Cardinal Kurt Koch's address from Rome, beneath the icon of Sts. Peter and Andrew. Twenty years after the dialogue resumed at Belgrade, the Cardinal called the agreed statements "a common heritage" and named the Orientale Lumen conference part of their reception.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Before the talks began, Cardinal O'Malley, Archbishop Pace, and Metropolitan Tikhon prayed vespers together in the Eastern form. In his keynote the Cardinal proposed joint Catholic and Orthodox pastoral letters on questions of shared moral concern, standing committees for common prayer, study, and works of mercy, and pointed to the Eastern Catholic Churches as a living model of communion without absorption. His charge to the room: "Come together and pray for unity."</p>
        <p>Archbishop Pace, on the panel: "The dream is that in the future, at the installation of the Pope, all the patriarchs of the Churches will be present."</p>
        <p>On the second day Archbishop Gabriele Caccia, Apostolic Nuncio to the United States, presented Jack Figel with the medal of Pope Leo XIV's first year, sent by the Holy Father.</p>
