      <h2>Thirty years</h2>
      <p class="dropcap">The first Orientale Lumen conference met in Washington in 1997, two years after John Paul II's apostolic letter of the same name asked the Western Church to learn again from the light of the East. It was organized by the United States chapter of the Society of St. John Chrysostom and published by Eastern Christian Publications, both under Jack Figel, and it has met every year since.</p>
      <p>The plenary speakers come from four traditions, Roman Catholic, Eastern Orthodox, Eastern Catholic, and Oriental Orthodox, and have included Metropolitan Kallistos Ware, Archimandrite Robert Taft, Archbishop Cyril Vasil', Archbishop Elpidophoros, Cardinal Kurt Koch by message, and a generation of the theologians who sit on the official dialogue. What makes the conference different is who else is in the room: lay people, and Eastern Catholics, who are a small minority in the Church and rarely represented where the dialogue is conducted.</p>
      <p>Alongside Washington the Foundation has held conferences in San Diego, Detroit, New Jersey, and Great Britain, and three Orientale Lumen EuroEast conferences in Constantinople at the invitation of the Ecumenical Patriarch, including a full day of sessions at the Halki Theological Academy.</p>
      <p>Every plenary since the beginning has been recorded. <a class="textlink" href="/oltv/">Watch them on OLTV →</a></p>
